The strongest new opening is control of an assembled atomic complex’s mobilityI have moved the main attack to post-growth atomic manufacture: changing where an existing defect complex can move inside a crystal while preserving its composition through the motion. The concrete testbed is a silicon–carbon divacancy in cubic silicon carbide, 3C-SiC.There is a substantive result: two published atomic rearrangements, coupled by ordinary charge exchange, produce long-range transport that is substantially faster than a calculation that keeps each complex in one charge state throughout its motion. I constructed the spatial kinetic model, calculated its diffusion coefficient, checked the result independently, and tested intermediate trapping and a competing chemical conversion.This is presently a source-grounded constitutive prediction and candidate manufacturing operation. It does not yet establish a superior positioning device. In particular, the improvement calculated below is a comparison between kinetic descriptions, not a measured advantage over conventional annealing.The bullvalene calculation, participation bound and reference-cycle criterion remain intact at their established scopes. This construction uses a different physical interface: the rearrangements directly move the defect through the host lattice.1. The mechanism has an actual atomic pathA divacancy, denoted VVVVVV, contains neighbouring silicon and carbon vacancies. Published calculations identify two relevant motions:A silicon atom moves so that the silicon vacancy changes position around a fixed carbon vacancy.Carbon rearrangement through a carbon-antisite intermediate moves the carbon vacancy around a fixed silicon vacancy.The charge-dependent barriers favour opposite motions:Charge of the complexSilicon-vacancy motion around carbon vacancyCarbon-vacancy motion through antisite intermediate+1+1+13.84 eV3.30 eV0003.23 eV3.79 eVThese are published 3C-SiC electronic-structure barriers. They are the physical ingredients of the construction, rather than rates chosen to produce a desired response. link.aps.orgEither motion alone confines the complex to a small region. Combining them permits translation.To see this explicitly, let the silicon-vacancy position initially be S=0S=0S=0, and the carbon-vacancy position be C=deiC=d\mathbf e_iC=dei​. Here ddd is the nearest-neighbour distance and the four ei\mathbf e_iei​ point along the tetrahedral bonds.A silicon-vacancy motion givesS′=d(ei−ej),C=dei.S'=d(\mathbf e_i-\mathbf e_j),\qquad C=d\mathbf e_i.S′=d(ei​−ej​),C=dei​.A subsequent carbon-vacancy motion can restore the original bond orientation:C′=S′+dei.C'=S'+d\mathbf e_i.C′=S′+dei​.The final object has the same composition and orientation as the initial object, but both vacancy positions have shifted byΔR=d(ei−ej).\Delta \mathbf R=d(\mathbf e_i-\mathbf e_j).ΔR=d(ei​−ej​).For the lattice constant used in the source calculation, this displacement has magnitude approximately 0.3120.3120.312 nm.This supplies the missing spatial operation. A completed internal cycle can translate the complex by a lattice step. Repeating only one kind of pivot cannot do that.The intermediate is chemically reconstructed: it contains a carbon antisite and two carbon vacancies before returning to a divacancy. Thus “preserving the complex” here means retaining and transporting its net missing-atom inventory, with recovery of the divacancy motif. It does not mean preserving an unchanged instantaneous structure or a coherent qubit state.2. The important coupling is between charge exchange and spatial correlationsA tempting calculation is to determine the diffusivity in each charge state and average those diffusivities. That loses the possibility that one charge state supplies one part of a translation cycle and another charge state supplies the other.I instead retained the four bond orientations, their spatial displacements and the charge state.Let aqa_qaq​ and bqb_qbq​ be the rates, per alternative 